% \maketitle
% \begin{abstract}
% An opentype font configuration package for Lua\LaTeXe{}.
% The aim is to provide an alternative to \pkg{fontspec} which is both faster and more powerful, by doing as much processing as possible in Lua.
% To avoid loading fonts unnecessarily, the data used is corrected during compilation and the results cached for future use.
%
% \emph{This package is experimental.}
% \end{abstract}
% 
% \section{Requirements}\label{sec:require}
%
% The package requires Lua\LaTeXe.
% It will not work with any other engine or format.
%
% Unicode maths is supported \emph{via} \pkg{lua-unicode-math}.
% It will \textbf{not} work with \pkg{unicode-math}.
%
% \section{Usage}\label{sec:pkg}
%
% \emph{The user interface is currently somewhat basic\footnote{%
%     \begin{tabular}{ll}
%       v0.1: & ‘\textbf{extremely} basic’.\\
%     \end{tabular}%
%   }%
% .}
%
% Currently documents using exclusively Latin scripts with no changes of the base font families are reasonably well supported \emph{via} key-value based configuration.
% This enables the choice of font family for the standard families --- i.e.~roman/serif, sans, typewriter/mono and (Unicode) maths (main, script and calligraphic) --- and configuration of additional fonts, again by family.
% In addition to this, scaling, ‘fake bold’, rendering mode, language choice and script selection may be configured for each family.
% A reasonable\footnote{%
%   Where ‘reasonable’ means I have some idea what they do and some, possibly merely theoretical, ability to test them.
%   This is naturally a quite unreasonable definition of ‘reasonable’ with a clear Western European bias.
%   Input from users of non-Latin scripts (initially for mono-script documents) would be particularly welcome.%
% } variety of opentype font features have key-value support.
%
% Alternatively or additionally, feature specifications may be passed more-or-less directly to the font loader.
% This enables any loader-supported specification to used and is faster, but requires knowledge of the syntax and opentype tag names.
%
% That said, the automated configuration is already quite extensive.
% By default, the package tries to configure font families which are as rich as possible, insofar as the fonts support this. 
% This includes
% \begin{itemize}
%   \item multiple widths and shapes, insofar as \textsc{nfss} supports them;
%   \item italic, oblique, upright italic, reverse italic, reverse oblique;
%   \item small-caps, italic small-caps, oblique small-caps\footnote{%
%     This should work whether small-caps are in separate fonts (e.g.~Latin Modern) or implemented by the \texttt{smcp} feature (e.g.~\TeX{}-Gyre Pagella).%
%   };
%   \item optical sizes.
% \end{itemize}
% Of course, this depends on the quality of the data the fonts provide.
% It is not always good or, even, vaguely accurate. 
% If things do not fit, some (currently minimal) effort is made to accommodate them as distinct font families, so they are still available at document level.
%
% At present, this is still a bit chaotic and probably not that easy to use, as the results are ‘hidden’ from you (in, of course, plain sight).
% Even so, you should typically need to do much less to access the fonts you want than you might otherwise expect. 
%
% The advantage of this is that if a font has several widths, you can easily adjust the one used for bold by simply redefining the relevant default.
% This is not very user friendly yet, but it is now a bit better than not completely terrible.
% 
%
% Simple usage looks like this:
% \iffalse
%<*verb>
% \fi
\begin{minted}{latex}
  \usepackage{lua-font-config}
  \fontconfig{%
    rm = texgyrepagella,
    sf = texgyreheros,
    tt = texgyrecursor,
    math = texgyrescholamath,
  }
\end{minted}
% \iffalse
%</verb>
% \fi
%
% \DescribeMacro{\fontconfig}\marg{key-val list}\par
% Specify font configuration.
% 
% \subsection{Keys}\label{subsec:keys}
% Top-level keys are described here.
% Keys for use in key-value lists are described in \cref{subsec:keys-cfg}.
%
% \DescribeKey{rm}\val{family name}\par
% \DescribeKey{rm}\mval{key-val list}\par
% Specify family to use as the main document font, typically a serif or ‘roman’ family.
%
% Available keys are listed below.
%
% \DescribeKey{sf}\val{family name}\par
% \DescribeKey{sf}\mval{key-val list}\par
% Specify family to use as the default sans family, typically a sans-serif.
%
% Available keys are listed below.
%
% \DescribeKey{tt}\val{family name}\par
% \DescribeKey{tt}\mval{key-val list}\par
% Specify family to use as the default ‘typewriter’ family, typically monowidth.
%
% Available keys are listed below.
%
% \DescribeKey{family}\val{family name}\par
% \DescribeKey{family}\mval{key-val list}\par
% Specify an additional text family.
% This will create a macro which can be used to switch to the requested family\footnote{%
%   Usually you will want to specify which macro, since otherwise you must figure out the family name the code uses, which may or may not match the one requested.
%   See \cref{subsubsec:keys-text-add}.%
% }.
%
% Available keys are listed below.
%
% \DescribeKey{math}\val{family name}\par
% \DescribeKey{math}\val{key-val list}\par
% Specify the family to use for typesetting Unicode mathematics.
% This sets the fonts used for symbols, script and calligraphic alphabets.
% It does not change the fonts used to typeset operators, since these are taken from text fonts and configured separately.
%
% Available keys are listed below.
%
% This option uses \pkg{lua-unicode-math}.
% \pkg{unicode-math} is \textbf{not} supported.
% If you wish to use \pkg{unicode-math}, do not use this option.
% Instead, load \pkg{unicode-math} and use \cs{setmathfont} as usual.
%
% \DescribeKey{debug}\mval{key-val list}\par
% This can be used to enable debugging and/or modify the logging level.
% It is primarily intended for use in debugging the package and unlikely to be of use in production.
% However, it may be useful the use this key if reporting a bug which cannot be reproduced e.g.~which is platform specific or depends on system fonts.
%
% \subsection{Keys for specifying font families}\label{subsec:keys-cfg}
%
% All keys described in \cref{subsec:keys} accept either a simple family name, as shown in the example above, or a braced key-value list.
% For example,
% \iffalse
%<*verb>
% \fi
\begin{minted}{latex}
  \usepackage{lua-font-config}
  \fontconfig{%
    rm = {%
      name = texgyrepagella, 
      fea  = {script=ltn;+tlig;+pnum},
    },
    sf = {%
      name  = texgyreheros,
      scale = 1.2,
      fea   = {script=cyrl;},
    },
    tt = {%
      name  = texgyrecursor,
      fea   = {+zero;},
      %% for completeness: false is anyway the default
      force = false,
    },
  }
\end{minted}
% \iffalse
%</verb>
% \fi
%
% This section describes these ‘inner’ keys.
%
% \subsubsection{Common keys}\label{subsubsec:common}
%
% These keys are accepted by all of \texttt{rm}, \texttt{sf}, \texttt{tt},
% \texttt{family} and \texttt{math}.
%
% \DescribeKey{name}\val{lookup name}\par
% The ‘name’ to lookup.
% This may, but need not be, the name of a font family.
% Shorter, less specific names will configure more families; longer, more specific names will configure fewer.
%
% For example, \texttt{name = texgyrepagellax,} will configure fewer than \texttt{name = texgyrepagella,} since the latter string matches both families, while the former does not.
% 
% The name is lower-cased prior to lookup. 
% The data used to generate actual font families uses a cleaned up version of the given name or, sometimes, a variation, depending on the data returned from the database.
%
% For example, \texttt{tex gyre PaGellA} yields the same results as \texttt{texgyrepagella}.
%
% Numbers are likewise removed\footnote{%
%   This is problematic for fonts which depend on digits in their names to distinguish them e.g.~Venturis ADF has four distinct ‘titling’ fonts, whose names differ by a single character, which is \texttt{1}, \texttt{2}, \texttt{3} or \texttt{4}. 
%   The algorithm used to sort the fonts into \textsc{nfss} families currently gets confused by this, leading to misleading warnings about ‘apparent duplicate’ fonts. 
%   (Something is indeed awry, but they are not duplicates.
%   Rather what should be four families appears to be only one.)%
% }.
%
%
% \DescribeKey{force}\vals{true,false}\par
% Boolean which determines whether cached data is to be regenerated on the basis of a new search of the font-loader's database.
%
% Note that this doesn't regenerate the loader's database itself. 
% It simply zaps data stored by this module for one or more font families.
%
% \DescribeKey{scale}\val{factor}\par
% Optional factor by which the font should be scaled e.g.~\texttt{20} or \texttt{0.8}.
% Ignored if a family includes distinct optical sizes.
%
% \subsubsection{Text-mode keys}\label{subsubsec:keys-text}
% These keys are accepted in all key-value specifications except for the top-level \texttt{math} key used to configure Unicode maths.
% They are used to configure font ‘features’ of various sorts.
% These include opentype features provided by the fonts, but also some additions provided by the font-loader or in other ways.
%
% There are currently two keys in this category.
% \texttt{fea} is a relatively low-level interface.
% It is fast, powerful and requires familiarity with opentype font features and the syntax supported by \pkg{luaotfload}.
% \texttt{features} is a mid-level interface which aims to provide a more user-friendly wrapper around \texttt{fea}.
%
% \textbf{If you are not sure which to use, use \texttt{features}.}
%
% The two keys may be used to configure the same family.
% If so, consistent options are combined; in case of conflicts, \texttt{features} takes precedence over \texttt{fea}.
% This means you can use the more-user friendly interface for most things and still add any unsupported specialist setup, if necessary. 
%
% \DescribeKey{features}\mval{key-value list}\par
% Optional list of font features \textbf{for text fonts only}. 
%
% The default is equivalent to \texttt{%
%   features=\{%
%     script=dflt,
%     mode=node,
%     language=dflt,
%     ligatures=tex%
%   \}%
% }.
% Unless provided in separate fonts, small-caps are enabled, if available, for upright, italic and oblique small-caps.
% 
% Features apply to \emph{all} fonts in the family specified by \texttt{name}, so you should not specify features you do not wish applied to all of them.
% 
% Currently, nine keys are supported.
% Where multiple values are listed, any combination of values is permitted in any order, but \textbf{inconsistent values should be avoided}.
%
% A \meta{key list} requires a comma-separated list of keys \emph{without values}:
%
% \texttt{\{\meta{key1},\meta{key2},\dots\}}
%
% A \meta{key-value list} requires a comma-separated list of key-values:
% \texttt{\{\meta{key1}=\meta{value1},\meta{key2}=\meta{value2},\dots\}}
%
% \DescribeKey{alternates}\mval{key/key-value list}\par
% \meta{key/key-value list} should be a comma-separate list of one or more of the following valueless keys\par
% \begin{tabularx}\linewidth{>{\ttfamily}lX}
%   contextual & enable standard contextual alternates \\
%   nocontextual & disable them \\
%   contextualswash & enable contextual ‘swash’ \\
%   swash & enable ‘swash’ \\
%   linefinal & enable end-of-line forms \\
%   wordfinal & enable end-of-word forms \\
%   justification & enable forms designed to help justify text \\
% \end{tabularx}\par
% and/or one or more of the following key-value pairs\par
% \begin{tabularx}\linewidth{>{\ttfamily}l@{\,\texttt{=}\,}lX}
%   annotation & \meta{integer} & enable ‘annotation’ form \meta{integer} \\
%   charactervariant & \meta{integer i}:\meta{integer j} & enable the \emph{i}th character mapping, selecting the \emph{j}th variant \\
%   stylistic & \meta{integer} & enable ‘stylistic’ form \meta{integer} \\
%   sset & \meta{integer} & enable the set of ‘stylistic’ forms \meta{integer} \\
% \end{tabularx}
%
% \DescribeKey{embolden}\val{factor}\par
% Add ‘fake bold’ to make the family heavier.
% Note that this applies to all fonts in the family; it is not intended to add fake bold to a family which lacks one.
%
% \DescribeKey{forms}\mval{key list}\par
% \meta{key list} should be a comma-separate list of one or more of the following valueless keys\par
% \begin{tabularx}\linewidth{>{\ttfamily}lX}
%   capstopetite  & map \texttt{ABC} to ‘petite’ caps \\
%   capstosc      & map \texttt{ABC} to small-caps \\
%   smalltopetite & map \texttt{abc} to ‘petite’ caps \\
%   case        & enable forms designed for use in capitalised contexts \\
%   nocase      & disable them \\
%   hist        & enable ‘historical’ forms of characters \\
%   historical  & \\
%   random      & characters vary pseudo-randomly \\
%   titling     & enable forms intended for use in titles \\
%   unicase     & map \texttt{ABCabc} to letters in a single-cased alphabet \\
% \end{tabularx}
%
% \DescribeKey{figures}\mval{key list}\par
% \meta{key list} should be a comma-separate list of one or more of the following valueless keys\par
% \begin{tabularx}\linewidth{>{\ttfamily}lX}
%   lining	      & use lining digits \\
%   oldstyle	    & use oldstyle / hanging digits \\
%   osf	          &  \\
%   prop	        & use proportional digits \\
%   proportional	&  \\
%   tab	          & use tabular digits \\
%   tabular	      &  \\
%   zero	        & use the slashed zero \\
% \end{tabularx}
%
% \DescribeKey{language}\val{language id}\par
% \meta{language id} must currently be a valid identifier, as recognised by \pkg{luaotfload}.
% \texttt{otfinfo -l }\meta{path to font file} may be used to determine identifiers supported by the font.
%
% \DescribeKey{ligatures}\mval{key list}\par
% \meta{key list} should be a comma-separate list of one or more of the following valueless keys\par
% \begin{tabularx}\linewidth{>{\ttfamily}lX}
%   contextual	  & enable ‘contextual’ ligatures \\    
%   nocontextual	& disable them \\                    
%   discretionary	& enable discretionary (non-standard) ligatures \\                   
%   hist	        & enable historical ligatures \\                            
%   historic	    &  \\                        
%   required	    & enable required ligatures \\                        
%   norequired	  & disable them \\                      
%   standard	    & enable standard (‘common’) ligatures \\                        
%   nostandard	  & disable them \\    
%   tex	          & enable \TeX{} ligatures e.g.~\texttt{--} \\           
%   notex	        & disable them \\         
% \end{tabularx}
%
% \DescribeKey{mode}\vals{base,mode,harf}\par
% Set the rendering mode.
%
% \DescribeKey{script}\val{script id}\par
% \meta{script id} must currently be a valid identifier, as recognised by \pkg{luaotfload}.
% \texttt{otfinfo -s }\meta{path to font file} may be used to determine identifiers supported by the font.
%
% \DescribeKey{spacing}\mval{key list}\par
% \meta{key list} should be a comma-separate list of one or more of the following valueless keys\par
% \begin{tabularx}\linewidth{>{\ttfamily}lX}
%   capsspacing   & enable spacing designed for capitalised text \\
%   kern          & enable kerning \\
%   nokern        & disable kerning \\
% \end{tabularx}
%
%
% \DescribeKey{fea}\mval{opentype feature spec.}\par
% Optional list of font features \textbf{for text fonts only}. 
% 
% Note that the specification is passed to the font loader with minimal processing, so you must use ‘raw’ feature specifications.
% \emph{If you do not know what these are, use the \texttt{features} key rather than this one; if you need an unsupported feature, file an issue on the bug tracker (link below).}
%
% The default is equivalent to \texttt{fea=\{mode=node;scrpt=dflt;language=dflt;+tlig\}}.
% Unless provided in separate fonts, \texttt{+smcp} is added, if available, for upright, italic and oblique small-caps.
% 
% Features apply to \emph{all} fonts configured as a result of the lookup, so you should not specify features you do not wish applied to all fonts to which the specification applies.
% 
% \subsubsection{Default text family keys}\label{subsubsec:keys-text-def}
% These keys are accepted by \texttt{rm}, \texttt{sf} and \texttt{tt} i.e.~when specifying the default text families for the document.
%
% These keys all do the same thing, so you may use your preferred syntactic sweetener. 
%
% \DescribeKey{math}\vals{true,false}\par
% \DescribeKey{maths}
% Should the current specification also be used for the corresponding text family in maths?
%
% For example, if you are configuring the main document family with \texttt{rm}, should that family also be used for operators, \cs{mathrm} etc.?
%
% The default is equivalent to \texttt{math = true}.
%
% \DescribeKey{nomath}
% \DescribeKey{nomaths}
% Equivalent to \texttt{math = false}.
%
% \subsubsection{Additional text family keys}\label{subsubsec:keys-text-add}
%
% \DescribeKey{command}|=\command|\par
% Use \cs{command} to switch to the font family, rather than creating a command from the family name.
% 
% By default the command to switch to the font family depends on the discovered family name i.e.~on the result of searching the database for the specified family.
% In many cases, this will be the value passed to the \texttt{name} key, but often it will not and sometimes this may not be the command name you want anyway.
% \texttt{command} allows you to override it.
%
% For example, the following would setup \cs{playfair} to switch to Playfair 
% Display, rather than the default choice of \cs{playfairdisplay}.
% \iffalse
%<*verb>
% \fi
